Unreleased (0.8.0):

- incompatible C API changes, the last planned; from 0.8.0 to 1.0.0 the
  API and ABI stay compatible:
  - spy_type() reports only the builtin data types and SPY_OBJECT
  - spy_set_step_limit() and spy_set_memory_limit() removed; use
    spy_set_limit() (also Go's SetStepLimit, Rust's set_step_limit)
- JavaScript package: no SharedArrayBuffer needed where JSPI exists;
  worker: false runs the engine on the calling thread
- spyc links libNAME.a archive modules
- spyc programs mount their directory, get sys.argv and exit as spy does
- added spy_set_argv(); spy passes its arguments into sys.argv
- Python-to-Python calls no longer use the C stack; deep recursion is bounded
  by the recursion limit alone
- added SPY_LIMIT_PAUSE, spy_paused() and spy_resume(): a spent budget or an
  interrupt can pause a call, which spy_resume() runs on
- calls of classes with a Python __init__, for loops over generators and
  Python iterators, and list, set and dict comprehensions no longer use the
  C stack
- sort keys, next(), send(), str(), repr(), __new__, properties, __getattr__
  and operator methods no longer use the C stack in most function code
- class bodies that call or loop, and with statements' __exit__, no longer
  use the C stack
- code compiled while pausing is set pauses inside operator, unary operator,
  subscript, augmented assignment, target list, del, format(), len(), int(),
  float(), hash(), f-string and every function's special methods
- min() and max() of a generator, and enumerate() or filter() over one,
  no longer use the C stack
- sorted() and list.sort() call a Python __lt__ without the C stack
- an instance in a list or tuple calls a Python __eq__ without the C stack
- reflected operator and comparison methods, after a number's or an
  instance's declines or before a base's, run without the C stack
- a list, set or dict comprehension over a name, list or tuple pauses in its
  body
- list(), sum(), sorted() and the other consumers, and target lists and
  starred display items in code compiled while pausing, take an instance's items through its Python
  __iter__ and __next__ without the C stack
- a call's starred argument takes a generator's or an instance's items
  without the C stack
- in and not in over a generator or an instance's Python __iter__ take
  its items without the C stack
- augmented assignment to an attribute (self.x += d) runs about twice as
  fast
- zip() and map() over generators take their items without the C stack
- filter() with a Python function, and dict() of a generator, run without the
  C stack
- frozenset() of a generator stops at an unhashable item, as set() does
- exec() and eval() of source run without the C stack; a trace function sees
  exec'd code's line events as CPython reports them
- getattr() with a default and hasattr() of a missing instance attribute run
  about three times as fast
- an import statement runs a module of source without the C stack, so a
  paused call can pause inside it
- a descriptor's Python __get__ and a class's own __getattribute__ run without
  the C stack
- a key's Python __hash__ runs without the C stack in dict and set displays,
  dict subscripts, stores and deletions, and in tests of a dict or set
- an instance's Python __index__ runs without the C stack when it indexes
  a list, tuple, str, bytes, bytearray or range
- a metaclass's Python __instancecheck__ and __subclasscheck__ run without
  the C stack in isinstance() and issubclass()
- a generator's close() runs its handlers without the C stack
- a dead instance's Python __del__ runs without the C stack
- a class statement's metaclass __new__ runs without the C stack
- a class statement's __set_name__ and __init_subclass__ calls run without
  the C stack
- an attribute store's Python __setattr__, property setter or __set__ runs
  without the C stack in function code
- code compiled while pausing is set pauses inside an attribute deletion's
  Python __delattr__, property deleter or __delete__
- yield from a generator resumes it without the C stack, about 7% faster
- abs(), round() and reversed() call an instance's Python __abs__,
  __round__ or __reversed__ without the C stack
- code compiled while pausing is set pauses inside the items' Python
  __repr__ in repr(), str(), print() and f-strings of a list or tuple
- raising and catching an exception runs about a third faster
- enumerate, filter, map and zip take their iterables' items again after
  ending, as CPython's do
- print() calls an instance's Python __str__ without the C stack in code
  compiled while pausing
- calling a class body's function directly returns its __class__ cell when
  the body loops or calls
- sorted() of a non-iterable raises "'X' object is not iterable"
- trace functions see a class body's first line as CPython reports it
- == on deeply nested containers no longer raises RecursionError
- a method call looks up its method before evaluating arguments that call
  code; an attribute read in an operator honors a property added later
- exception classes have CPython's own __str__, __repr__ and __new__
- generator functions run about 10% faster (generators benchmark 99 ms -> 88 ms)
- raise no longer calls the exception's __str__; the host reads the message
  when the call returns, and raising and catching run about a third faster
- calls of functions with *args or **kwargs run up to twice as fast (with
  statements a third faster)
- calling an instance's special method by name (p.__lt__(q), self.__init__())
  runs about 2.5 times as fast
- hasattr() and getattr() with a default on a missing attribute run about 2.7
  times as fast
- sorting strs runs about 40% faster

2026-11-05 0.7.0:

- the cycle collector runs automatically (SPY_LIMIT_COLLECTION; zero
  leaves it to spy_collect) and is about twice as fast on a large heap
- removed the gc module
- sorting is 3.6x faster (sort benchmark 128 ms -> 35 ms)
- tuples store their items inline
- Go, Rust, Java and the worker protocol load native modules

2026-11-03 0.6.0:

- incompatible C API changes, the last before 1.0:
  - spy_truth() takes the context and returns -1 when __bool__ or
    __len__ fails; Go's Value.Truth and Rust's Value::truth return the
    error
  - spy_set_output() and spy_set_error_output() take the stream's flush
    callback; spy_set_flush() is removed, and sys.stderr.flush() reaches
    the error stream's flush
  - SPYModuleLoader and SPYReadLine receive the context and return owned
    values (bytes source, str line) instead of malloc buffers, so host
    and engine never share an allocator
  - SPYWrite and SPYFlush receive the context first, as every callback
    but the interrupt and release do; a failing one may set its own error
- the stability promise covers spy.h; the contents of _spy are outside it

2026-11-03 0.5.0:

- step budgets, memory limits and an interrupt callback; the C API and
  packages report the steps a call spent
- every engine line runs under the tests; CI enforces coverage
- every C API declaration documented, ABI compatibility checked
- CPython 3.15.2 is the oracle; 26 more CPython language tests ported
  (72 in all)
- error messages, argument errors and keyword suggestions follow CPython
- str, bytes, tuple and dict subclasses accepted where builtins expect
  their base type
- spy, spyc and spy-worker binaries published per release tag
- faster than CPython 3.15 on loop, bigint, dicts, strings and classes,
  within 4% on sort, nbody and generators
- embedding lifecycle in 186 us (was 192 us)

2026-10-31 0.4.0:

- faster than CPython 3.15 on loop, bigint, dicts and classes, within 13%
  on the other benchmark programs
- values allocated from pools in three size classes
- small numbers and short str and bytes stored inline
- reused function environments and cached name lookups
- generator bodies run more of their work as machine instructions
- embedding lifecycle in 192 us (was 257 us)

2026-10-31 0.3.0:

- added Rust crates and a Go module
- spyc: bundle imported modules into the C output (-m, -I, -x, --alias,
  --metafile, --deps, --analyze, --minify, ...)
- added spy_set_optimize(); spy: added -O and -OO
- sys and gc follow CPython 3.15 (settrace, setprofile, excepthook,
  unraisablehook, lazy imports, gc counts and freezing)
- added spy_set_error_output()
- removed the weakref module
- added allocation failure, ThreadSanitizer and coverage tests, and a
  spyc fuzzer
- added the flappy, pong and todo examples
- Cython baseline: 383 static and 407 dynamic examples pass

2026-10-29 0.2.0:

- faster name lookup, builtin dispatch and attribute access
- faster dicts, sets, list copies, sort and non-ASCII strings
- Karatsuba multiplication and Knuth division for big integers
- 'x in bytes' accepts any bytes-like needle
- Unicode tables in the libunicode encoding (732 KB to 343 KB)

2026-10-26 0.1.0:

- first release: C11 interpreter with the spy_* embedding API
- reference counting and a cycle collector
- spy interpreter and spyc C source generator
- arbitrary precision integers, complex numbers, exact float rounding
- classes, C3 MRO, super, metaclasses, descriptors, __slots__
- generators, coroutines, async generators and comprehensions
- pattern matching, f-strings, template strings, PEP 563 and PEP 649
  annotations, lazy imports (PEP 810)
- frozendict, memoryview and the buffer protocol (PEP 688)
- %-formatting, str.format and the format mini-language ported from CPython
- Unicode 17 identifiers, names, properties and NFKC normalization
- PEP 263 source encodings
- package, relative and namespace package imports, import hooks
- open() restricted to directories mounted with spy_mount()
- CPython 3.15 error messages and source ranges
- standard modules moved out of the core (spy_new_module()); the
  standard layer spy-std.c supplies sys and gc
- added spy_set_opaque(), spy_set_input() and spy_collect()
- added the small-python Python package
- PLS-315, the language standard, with spec tests and TLA+ models for
  every clause
- ported CPython language tests in tests/spec/ports
- AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer runs
